{"id": "unknown-2026-11-18-007", "date": "2026-11-18", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 11, "week": 47, "weekday": "Wed"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["module_price", "industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": ["[否定]pressure", "delay"]}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "European solar module prices face additional pressure from surplus stock, cancelled projects", "content": "<p>In a chat with <b>pv magazine</b>, Bart Wansink, CEO of Netherlands-based Search4Solar, discussed how surplus modules from cancelled projects, high inventory levels and Chinese manufacturing reforms are shaping European solar module prices. He expects prices to stabilise in 2027, although persistent oversupply could delay a sustained recovery.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/11/18/european-solar-module-prices-face-additional-pressure-from-surplus-stock-cancelled-proj", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/11/18/european-solar-module-prices-face-additional-pressure-from-surplus-stock-cancelled-projects/", "type": "rss"}]}
